{Nimzo-Indian Defense: Normal Variation, Bernstein Defense, Exchange Line. A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Nf3 c5 {Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.} 6. Bd3 d5 7. O-O Nc6 8. a3 ! Bxc3 {The critical moment. The pieces are as good as they are going to get; something has to happen now.} (8... Bxa3 Rb1 Bxb2 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. bxc3 Qc7 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Nh4 Bd7 ?! 12. f3 Rfe8 13. g4 Rac8 14. Ra2 cxd4 15. cxd4 g6 16. Ng2 Na5 17. a4 a6 18. Nf4 Nc4 19. Qe1 Qd6 20. Kh1 b5 21. axb5 Bxb5 22. Rg1 Rc6 23. Bb1 Rc7 24. Qf2 Rce7 25. Qh4 Rxe3 26. g5 Nd7 27. Bxe3 Nxe3 28. Bd3 Nf8 29. Bxb5 axb5 30. Nd3 Nf5 31. Qf4 Qd8 32. Ne5 Ne6 33. Qd2 Nexd4 34. f4 Qd6 35. Ra7 Qc5 36. Rd7 Rf8 37. Ng4 b4 38. Nh6+ Nxh6 39. gxh6 Nf5 40. Qxd5 Qe3 {The rest is technique, which is another way of saying it still has to be played accurately.} 41. Qxf5 1/2-1/2
